 "total_ms": 870.259199999964, "queue_wait_ms": 0.1, "start_ms": 1418.6, "cancelled": false}], "events": [{"at_ms": 0.0, "name": "client", "patience_s": 120.0}, {"at_ms": 0.1, "name": "papers_named", "papers": ["hnet"]}, {"at_ms": 2291.8, "name": "verified", "round": 0, "refused": false, "kept": 5, "dropped": []}], "status": "ok", "outcome": "answered", "first_token_ms": 2291.8354999997064, "total_ms": 2291.8464999997923, "retries": 0, "regenerations": 0, "tokens_in": 1671, "tokens_out": 261, "degraded": false, "queue_wait_ms": 0.05210000017541461, "queue_len_at_arrival": 0, "answer": "H\u2011Net\u2019s encoder and decoder networks are built from a stack of sequence\u2011mixing layers, specifically Mamba\u20112 layers, which are a type of state\u2011space model [1][2]. The choice of Mamba\u20112 is driven by its ability to handle fine\u2011grained, uncompressed sequences efficiently, a key requirement for both preserving residual detail and compressing inputs for the main network [2]. Mamba\u20112 layers also provide substantial computational efficiency when processing long sequences, meeting the design constraints of the encoder and decoder that must operate on uncompressed data [2]. In the experimental setups, the outer encoder/decoder networks are explicitly switched to Mamba\u20112, and the architecture is tuned to match the overall H\u2011Net models, confirming the central role of Mamba\u20112 in these components [3]. Thus, H\u2011Net uses Mamba\u20112 layers in its encoder and decoder to effectively balance fine\u2011grained information preservation, compression for the main network, and computational efficiency [1][2][3].", "citations": ["[1] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a72.1.1 Components of H-Net, pp.4-5", "[2] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a72.1.2 Design Principles, p.5", "[3] H-Net (arXiv 2507.07955v2), \u00a73 Experiments, pp.10-11"], "answer_grade": ""}
