al_ms": 577.7846999999383, "queue_wait_ms": 0.3, "start_ms": 1175.9, "cancelled": false}], "events": [{"at_ms": 0.0, "name": "client", "patience_s": 120.0}, {"at_ms": 0.1, "name": "papers_named", "papers": ["moshi"]}, {"at_ms": 1756.4, "name": "verified", "round": 0, "refused": false, "kept": 4, "dropped": ["no citation"]}], "status": "ok", "outcome": "answered_partial", "first_token_ms": 1756.4463000003343, "total_ms": 1756.4573000004202, "retries": 0, "regenerations": 0, "tokens_in": 957, "tokens_out": 256, "degraded": false, "queue_wait_ms": 0.27949999957854743, "queue_len_at_arrival": 0, "answer": "Inner Monologue is a training and inference setup for Moshi that decomposes speech into a chain of text, semantic and acoustic tokens and predicts this structured sequence in a hierarchical manner, allowing the model to represent all utterances as both text and speech rather than switching modalities [1][2]. By integrating acoustic tokens into the same generative model, it enables generating arbitrary voices and conditions instead of a single speaker, and the hierarchical modeling lets Moshi decompose the generation task without increasing the Transformer sequence length [1]. Because Inner Monologue predicts time\u2011aligned text tokens before audio tokens, it significantly improves the linguistic quality and factuality of generated speech and allows deriving streaming TTS and ASR systems from a single Moshi model [1][2]. The per\u2011frame prediction of speech tokens makes Moshi compatible with real\u2011time generation, achieving a theoretical latency of 160\u202fms and practical latency of 200\u202fms for full\u2011duplex spoken dialogue [1][3].\n\n(1 statement(s) were removed because the cited passages did not support them.)", "citations": ["[1] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a72 Related Work, p.5", "[2] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a71 Introduction, p.3", "[3] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a7Abstract, p.1"], "answer_grade": ""}
