al_ms": 939.330599999721, "queue_wait_ms": 0.0, "start_ms": 1242.6, "cancelled": false}], "events": [{"at_ms": 0.0, "name": "client", "patience_s": 120.0}, {"at_ms": 0.1, "name": "papers_named", "papers": ["moshi"]}, {"at_ms": 2185.0, "name": "verified", "round": 0, "refused": false, "kept": 5, "dropped": []}], "status": "ok", "outcome": "answered", "first_token_ms": 2185.0139999996827, "total_ms": 2185.029400000076, "retries": 0, "regenerations": 0, "tokens_in": 957, "tokens_out": 250, "degraded": false, "queue_wait_ms": 0.038500000300700776, "queue_len_at_arrival": 0, "answer": "Inner Monologue is a training and inference setup that decomposes speech into a chain of text, semantic and acoustic tokens and predicts this structured sequence in a hierarchical manner, allowing the model to represent all utterances as both text and speech rather than switching modalities [1][2]. By integrating acoustic tokens into the same generative model, it enables generating arbitrary voices and conditions instead of a single speaker, and it decomposes the generation task without increasing the Transformer sequence length, which improves efficiency [1]. Because Inner Monologue predicts time\u2011aligned text tokens before audio tokens, it significantly improves the factuality and linguistic quality of generated speech and allows deriving streaming TTS and ASR systems from Moshi by forcing a delay between token types [2][3]. This approach also makes Moshi compatible with real\u2011time generation, as each prediction step outputs a speech frame on a per\u2011frame basis, unlike methods that require generating a complete text sequence first [1]. The method is used to achieve a real\u2011time full\u2011duplex spoken large language model with low latency and high linguistic quality [4].", "citations": ["[1] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a72 Related Work, p.5", "[2] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a71 Introduction, p.3", "[3] Moshi (arXiv 2410.00037v1), \u00a73.4.4 Inner Monologue, p.17